fied in order to provide a piece of  data and all that data pulled together  helps us find things out and learn  things and make decisions most of you  probably know what GPS is or Google  Earth or use nav systems in your car  those are investments that nga has made  throughout our history  that the commercial side has turned into  commercial viable commercially viable  products but we use to bring in data to  our agency among those in addition to  Google Earth and GPS our commercial  imagery companies like digital globe  that a lot of people use to provide to  you to find unclassified data to help  make decisions so we support national  security and humanitarian efforts I'll  give you a couple examples of how things  happened a while ago talking ten plus  years ago that have also helped shape my  mindset and helped me try to drive  towards a different approach one example  is when we after 9/11 we had units going  in and out of countries conducting  operations and the intelligence units  that went in to support them would come  in with their own data drop in for a  period of time six months to a year and  then leave with their data another unit  would come back in and essentially  wiping the history of what happened in  that place over the course of that six  months or year for the next unit that  was coming in and they had to start all  over because we couldn't find a way to  make the data accessible to everyone to  allow us to make smarter decisions or  understand things over a longer period  of time so through technology and  architecture we were able to solve that  solution or solve that problem another  example is the Ebola crisis which I  talked about earlier so we worked with  the units that were going over there to  support from the Department of Defense  side to help them understand where were  the best places to put the Ebola  treatment units in order to make sure we  were having the biggest impact possible  on that crisis that required us to look  at a lot of data in order to make those  decisions and because of that event in  particular our director Robert Cardillo  in 2014 for the first time made all of  our data available on the World Wide Web  so that marks a significant shift in our  agency's approach to be able to put  things out on a completely unclassified  environment  the reason we needed to do that is there  are so many people let me change that  not needed to do that preferred to and  chose to do that is there were so many  people that needed that information that  didn't have access to our architecture  and we didn't have access to their data  and this just left a huge gap from a  mission perspective in making sure we  were able to support the needs of the  humanitarian organizations the  non-governmental organizations Red Cross  and those types of organizations to be  able to make the best decisions so we  have a lot of data within the agency and  a lot of data coming in all the time as  a matter of fact the amount of data  that's coming in is getting to be more  than we can possibly happen handle we've  moved from hunting within one image for  the perfect piece of information to help  us about that moment in time and that  place on earth to trying to create  coherence from a flood of data that's  coming in all the time through different  sources some of it classified and some  of it unclassified but it's a lot of  data and we're starting to think about  how we develop models of things that  happen around the world in order to give  predictive and be able to address a  problem before it happens so how much  data are we talking about at this we  have a conference once a year called the  geo and symposium and our director spoke  there last month and he believes we're  standing where the Sigyn community stood  when the internet became the digital  fabric of the planet we really think  geospatial information is about to be  that next fabric of the planet in how we  approach data and whether it's a  persistent view from space or air or  ground or even the sea in five years  there's probably going to be a million  times more data than we have right now  and are unable to get through all of it  right now  so just for comparison's sakes if we  were to try to manually look at all the  data all the commercial satellite data  that's coming in today and that we  expect to have over the next 20 years we  would need 8 million  to take a look at that that's a lot more  than we'll ever have so we need to find  a way to automate things and find other  technology solutions and that's all in  HD we have so much data right now we  can't take advantage of it all we simply  can't look at it all we can't organize  it all we can't visualize at all we  can't use all the data that we have so  we have to focus the resources we have  on the very hardest problems and find  ways to work with other organizations so  it's a nexus for innovation for us on  how we manage and use our data to answer  analytic questions we have taken some  steps in structuring and standardizing  datasets and spatially enabling them  from non geo and sources such as open  source information new press and those  kinds of things large integrated  multi-source data enables types of  correlation alerting and forensic  analysis that has resulted in  discoveries of new new systems new  insight and new detail on relatively  well-known targets and an ability to  ferret out patterns of life and  observable activities of some of the  hardest intelligence targets however  even though we've made progress in  structuring and standardizing the  datasets we still haven't realized the  full extent of what big data analysis  can do for us we need to spend more on  algorithm development on automation this  has been a decades-long problem for us  that we've been trying to solve with  significant investment and get to  spatiotemporal processing of remote  sensing data that allows our analysts to  focus on the really difficult challenges  we're working with key partners in  academia and several universities as  well as in R&amp;D organizations to try to  achieve this what we'd like to do is  distill down all the data into  meaningful insights predictions and  other bits of information this isn't  unique to the intelligence community  we've heard speakers this morning talk  about how they're trying to do that for  investments for their organizations for  how to help match people to a vacation  home etc they're all the same problems  when it comes to the data perspective  ultimately we'd like to make the systems  a tool for our analysts so they don't  have to spend so much time working on  the data and getting it right but using  the data to answer questions make have  easier and better tools for interacting  with the data open up our systems so we  they have more access to data across the  government in fact about a two years ago  Senator Warner from Virginia wrote a  letter about how just basically screwed  up the geospatial information is across  the government now I'm not talking  specifically about our agency this is  government-wide a lot of municipal and  state governments have done a good job  of bringing their data together to help  make decisions on infrastructure systems  and investment decisions from a state  and local budget perspective but within  the federal government because there are  so many different agencies with so many  different stores of data that is that's  all treated and handled and used in  different ways it really is kind of a  mess that's one way to put it I describe  it as a huge opportunity for us to be  able to bring all that data together and  help the government make better  decisions we need systems and algorithms  to correlate that data and find needles  in haystacks and the capabilities that  go beyond and answer specific questions  the cultural challenges are the hardest  and I think even at our own agency  they're the hardest but I think we're  ahead of everybody else because our  leadership has made investments in these  capabilities including what I mentioned  earlier some pilot projects with  containerization and approaches to data  science it's going to take a big effort  to change this culture though and be  able to use a multiple a multitude of  sources and large volumes of data we're  investing a lot in training and  resources for our analysts to fill my  eyes them with and equip them to make  sense of all the data but we're not  going to be able to tackle those big  data problems alone we need close  partners with industry academia and  research and development organizations  now most of what I've talked about so  far has been what we call about what we  call mission data so that's the imagery  or  data about things that happen in places  on the earth but we also have a  challenge within government and within  our own agency on what I call the  corporate data side so this is how do  you use the dollars that the taxpayers  have given us through Congress to the  greatest effect and how do you use the  people who work in the agency to the  greatest effect so we've actually  started structuring all of our corporate  data in a way that helps us make smarter  decisions and determine what the return  on investment is from those decisions  and we've seen some change some shifts  in our leadership and how they make  those decisions from listening to people  who have really good sales pitches about  really good potential outcomes based on  investment to listening to our data  folks who have pulled the data together  in a way that those decision-makers  haven't seen before we use tableau a  fair amount for a lot of our HR  decisions and we're starting to look at  different ways to look at our financial  decisions using different tools so we're  trying to start a new conversation  across different entities to make sure  we're getting the best and the brightest  input that doesn't necessarily mean  people coming to work for 