so take in all  kinds of other form of data to try to  determine what's happening at a specific  place at a specific time and answer some  questions about for example in the news  this weekend right North Korea launched  a missile so we're trying to make sure  we're preventing our enemies from being  able to have a capability to strike the  United States that's one of our mission  areas I mentioned the Ebola mission  early earlier so one of our other key  missions is understanding the earth and  what that usually has to do with is what  we call safety of navigation so making  sure aircraft and ships and vehicles can  transit wherever they need to transit  safely without running into each other  or things etc and so in the case of the  Ebola mission we used that side of our  mission area to understand where the  Ebola treatment units could go you know  what the terrain was like so how fast  could people get to the units if they  got sick those kinds of things so and we  do work on a number of different domains  most of them are internal domains to the  agency or to the intelligence community  so did I answer your question yeah so I  would say we have some folks here who  are smarter than I am on that so I'll  ask him to correct me but probably 90  plus percent of our data currently is  kept in a classified domain 90 plus  percent however that doesn't mean that  much of that data is classified and  that's what I was trying to get at  through this discussion is I think a lot  of that data can actually be openly  shared it's because of security policies  and regulations and laws that we aren't  able to do that right now  but we have started challenging those  laws and policies in a way that allowed  us for example during the Ebola crisis  to share information out on the World  Wide Web so I'm kind of curious about  collaboration between you all and the  other intelligence agencies do you do  the other are the other intelligence  agencies more customers of both like the 